## 1. Technical angle

AI surrogates for physics are fast. The hard part is knowing when one is wrong.

I built PhysicsVeto, a small workbench that wraps a Fourier Neural Operator for 2D seismic wave propagation in
a "physics veto." Every prediction is checked, without the true answer, against:
• the wave equation itself (finite-difference residual)
• the absorbing boundary condition
• energy conservation after the source stops
• distance from the training data (Mahalanobis)
• agreement with an independently trained U-Net

On a planted-failure benchmark (salt bodies, extreme contrast, rough geology, moved shots, injected
artifacts), the veto stopped {{flag_r}} of truly bad predictions, and the share of bad answers among accepted
predictions fell from {{raw_bad}} to {{acc_bad}}.

What surprised me most was a blind spot. A wavefield that is uniformly scaled almost satisfies a linear wave
equation, so the residual check barely notices it. What caught it was disagreement with a second,
independently trained model. Reporting results per failure mode makes that dependency visible.

Also measured honestly: at 64×64 on a CPU the solver is faster than the network. The crossover shows up as
resolution grows.

