## 2. Business angle

The number that decides whether AI surrogates pay off in simulation isn't the speedup. It's the fallback rate.

If 10% of surrogate predictions have to be re-run on the full solver, the whole workflow can never be more than
10× faster, whether the model is 100× or 1,000× faster. Effective speedup = 1 / (veto rate + 1/speedup).

So I built the ROI model in PhysicsVeto to subtract every vetoed run, using the veto rate it actually measures
({{vr}} on normal work in the demo case), with a tornado chart showing what really moves the business case.
The answer: veto rate and problem size, more than model speed.

For CAE and HPC teams, that changes the roadmap. Investing in active learning on vetoed cases beats chasing a
faster network.

